9) You suspect a memory leak. How will you confirm it? — Ready-to-Speak

“If I suspect a memory leak, I first look at memory usage over time.

If memory keeps increasing and is not reclaimed even after full GC cycles, it indicates a leak rather than normal usage.

Then I capture heap dumps at different intervals and compare them to identify objects that are continuously growing.

I analyze retained objects and reference chains to find what is preventing garbage collection.

I also check common sources like unbounded caches, static collections, or listeners not being deregistered.

One important edge case is cache misconfiguration where data is intentionally retained but behaves like a leak.

So overall, I confirm a leak using memory trends and heap dump analysis instead of just relying on symptoms.”


Follow-ups

