11) You see deadlock in your system. How will you detect and resolve it? — Ready-to-Speak

“Deadlock occurs when two or more threads wait on each other’s locks, causing the system to stop progressing.

I detect it by capturing thread dumps and looking for circular lock dependencies, where each thread is waiting for a lock held by another.

Then I analyze the code paths to identify nested locking or inconsistent lock ordering.

To fix it, I enforce a consistent lock ordering, reduce nested locks, or replace blocking locks with tryLock and timeouts.

I may also redesign critical sections to reduce shared locking or use concurrent utilities.

One important edge case is intermittent deadlocks that only occur under specific timing conditions.

So overall, I detect deadlocks using thread dumps and resolve them by improving locking strategy and reducing contention.”


Follow-ups

