Q: “What is lock ordering?”
A: “Always acquiring locks in a consistent sequence to avoid circular waits.”

Q: “How does tryLock help?”
A: “It avoids indefinite waiting by failing fast if lock is not available.”

Q: “Can deadlocks be intermittent?”
A: “Yes, they may occur only under specific timing or load conditions.”

Q: “How do you prevent deadlocks?”
A: “Avoid nested locks, use lock ordering, and minimize shared resources.”

Q: “Alternative to locks?”
A: “Use concurrent data structures or redesign to reduce shared state.”



