15) Your thread pool gets exhausted under load. What's your approach? — Ready-to-Speak

“If a thread pool gets exhausted under load, it usually means tasks are blocking or arriving faster than they’re processed.

I first identify why threads are busy—typically due to blocking I/O like slow DB or external calls, or missing timeouts—using thread dumps and APM.

Then I reduce blocking by adding timeouts, optimizing slow dependencies, and using async or non-blocking approaches where possible.

I also tune thread pool size and queue capacity based on workload, and isolate workloads using bulkheads so one slow dependency doesn’t exhaust all threads.

For I/O-heavy workloads, I may use virtual threads to handle high concurrency more efficiently.

One important edge case is retry storms, where aggressive retries amplify load and exhaust threads quickly.

So overall, I remove blocking, control concurrency, and apply proper isolation instead of just increasing thread count.”


Follow-ups

Q: “How do you confirm thread pool exhaustion?”
A: “High latency, increasing queue size, maxed-out threads, and rejected executions.”

Q: “Why not just increase thread count?”
A: “It increases CPU and memory pressure and doesn’t solve blocking issues.”

Q: “What tools help debug this?”
A: “Thread dumps, APM, and metrics like pool utilization and queue size.”

Q: “How do timeouts help?”
A: “They prevent threads from waiting indefinitely on slow dependencies.”

Q: “What is bulkhead isolation?”
A: “Separate thread pools per dependency to avoid resource starvation.”

Q: “When would you use virtual threads?”
A: “For I/O-bound workloads where many threads spend time waiting.”



16) You need to handle high concurrency safely. What will you use? — Ready-to-Speak

