17) Your system processes duplicate requests. How will you handle it? — Ready-to-Speak

“Idempotency ensures that even if the same request is received multiple times, the system produces only one side effect and returns the same response.

I handle it at three levels.

First, at the API level using an idempotency key—if the key already exists, I return the stored response instead of reprocessing.

Second, at the database level using unique constraints like transaction ID to prevent duplicates across instances.

Third, I ensure atomicity so concurrent duplicate requests don’t pass the check together, using transactions or mechanisms like Redis SETNX.

One important edge case is partial failure—like a payment succeeded but response failed—so retries should return the original success instead of creating duplicates.

So overall, I combine API-level deduplication with strong persistence guarantees to safely handle retries.”


Follow-ups

Q: “What if two requests come at the same time?”
A: “Atomicity ensures only one succeeds—using DB constraints or Redis SETNX.”

Q: “Is idempotency key alone enough?”
A: “No, DB constraints act as a final safety net.”

Q: “What if payload differs for same key?”
A: “Reject the request to prevent inconsistency.”

Q: “How long do you store idempotency keys?”
A: “Using TTL like 24–48 hours based on business needs.”

Q: “Why store response?”
A: “To return consistent response for retries without reprocessing.”

Q: “Where do you store idempotency data?”
A: “DB or distributed cache like Redis depending on scale and latency.”




18) A cache is giving stale data. How will you fix it? — Ready-to-Speak

“If a cache is giving stale data, it means the cache lifecycle or invalidation strategy is not aligned with data updates.

I first identify the caching strategy—whether it’s read-through, write-through, or write-behind—and how updates propagate.

Then I fix it by applying proper invalidation, like TTL-based expiry or event-driven cache eviction when underlying data changes.

I also ensure consistency between cache and database, especially in distributed systems where multiple instances update data.

For critical data, I may use cache-aside with explicit invalidation on writes.

One important edge case is cache stampede, where many requests hit the DB after cache expiry.

So overall, I fix stale data by aligning cache invalidation with data updates and choosing the right caching strategy.”


Follow-ups

Q: “What is the most common cause of stale cache?”
A: “Missing or delayed cache invalidation after data update.”

Q: “TTL vs event-driven invalidation?”
A: “TTL is simpler but less precise; event-driven is accurate but more complex.”

Q: “What is cache-aside?”
A: “Application loads data from DB and updates cache manually.”

Q: “What is cache stampede?”
A: “Multiple requests hit DB simultaneously when cache expires.”

Q: “How do you prevent stampede?”
A: “Use locking, request coalescing, or staggered TTL.”

Q: “When to avoid caching?”
A: “Highly dynamic or critical real-time data.”


19) Your application is not scaling even after adding instances. Why? — Ready-to-Speak

“If an application is not scaling even after adding instances, it usually means there is a bottleneck in a shared resource or the system is not truly stateless.

I first check shared dependencies like database, cache, or external services, because they can become bottlenecks under increased load.

Then I verify load distribution—whether traffic is evenly balanced across instances or if there are hotspots.

I also check if the application is stateful—for example, session stickiness or in-memory state can prevent proper scaling.

Next, I analyze resource limits like DB connection pools or thread pools that may not scale with instances.

One important edge case is a single point of contention like a synchronized block or global lock in the application.

So overall, I identify shared bottlenecks and ensure stateless, properly distributed architecture for true scalability.”


Follow-ups

